---
import data from '../data/inventory-example.json';
import ResponsiveTable from './ResponsiveTable.astro';
const removal = -data.units * data.disposalCostPerUnit;
const liquidation =
  data.units * (data.liquidationReceiptPerUnit - data.liquidationCostPerUnit);
const scenarios = data.scenarios.map((s) => ({
  ...s,
  unsold: data.units - s.soldUnits,
  sale:
    s.soldUnits * (data.saleReceiptPerUnit - data.saleCostPerUnit) -
    (data.units - s.soldUnits) * data.disposalCostPerUnit,
}));
---

<div class="inventory-example">
  <p>
    <strong>All figures and assumptions are fictional.</strong> This general
    decision-support example does not reproduce the employer workbook. Values use invented
    cash units, not a real currency.
  </p>
  <h3>One fictional lot, two possible outcomes</h3>
  <p>
    Both scenarios start with {data.units} fictional units of Product Alpha and use the
    same hypothetical decision window. Only assumed discounted-sale demand changes. All
    options finish with no remaining stock: any units not sold in that window are disposed
    of.
  </p>
  <ResponsiveTable label="Fictional shared assumptions">
    <table>
      <caption>Shared assumptions · invented cash units</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Assumption</th>
          <th scope="col">Value</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Starting stock</td>
          <td>{data.units} units</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Discounted-sale receipt per sold unit</td>
          <td>{data.saleReceiptPerUnit}</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Sale handling cost per sold unit</td>
          <td>{data.saleCostPerUnit}</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Disposal cost per unit</td>
          <td>{data.disposalCostPerUnit}</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Liquidation receipt per unit</td>
          <td>{data.liquidationReceiptPerUnit}</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Liquidation handling cost per unit</td>
          <td>{data.liquidationCostPerUnit}</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </ResponsiveTable>
  <p class="caption">
    Net cash here means future receipts minus the displayed future costs. Original
    purchase cost is treated as already incurred and identical across options; taxes,
    timing, storage costs and other effects are deliberately omitted. This is not a profit
    estimate or a realistic valuation model. Liquidation is assumed to accept the entire
    lot; these assumptions would need verification in a real decision.
  </p>
  {scenarios.map((s) => (
    <section aria-labelledby={s.label.replace(' ', '-')} class="inventory-scenario">
      <h3 id={s.label.replace(' ', '-')}>
        {s.label} · {s.soldUnits} units sell
      </h3>
      <p>
        Assumed discounted-sale demand: {s.soldUnits} units. Remaining stock for disposal:{' '}
        {data.units} − {s.soldUnits} = {s.unsold} units.
      </p>
      <ResponsiveTable
        label={s.label + ' fictional option calculations; stacked records on mobile'}
      >
        <table class="mobile-records" role="table">
          <caption>{s.label} · independently checkable net cash</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Option</th>
              <th scope="col">Calculation</th>
              <th scope="col">Net cash</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td data-label="Option">Removal/disposal</td>
              <td data-label="Calculation">
                0 − {data.units} × {data.disposalCostPerUnit}
              </td>
              <td data-label="Net cash">{removal}</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td data-label="Option">Discounted sale</td>
              <td data-label="Calculation">
                {s.soldUnits} × ({data.saleReceiptPerUnit} − {data.saleCostPerUnit}) −{' '}
                {s.unsold} × {data.disposalCostPerUnit}
              </td>
              <td data-label="Net cash">{s.sale}</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td data-label="Option">Liquidation</td>
              <td data-label="Calculation">
                {data.units} × ({data.liquidationReceiptPerUnit} −{' '}
                {data.liquidationCostPerUnit})
              </td>
              <td data-label="Net cash">{liquidation}</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </ResponsiveTable>
      <p class="scenario-result">
        <strong>
          Calculated preference:{' '}
          {s.sale > liquidation ? 'discounted sale' : 'liquidation'}.
        </strong>{' '}
        Highest net cash among the displayed options under this scenario's assumptions.
      </p>
    </section>
  ))}
  <h3>What changed, and what did not</h3>
  <p>
    Changing assumed sales from 8 to 3 units changes the discounted-sale result from 52 to
    7. Liquidation remains 20, so the calculated preference switches. Removal/disposal
    remains −20. These are fictional arithmetic results, not employer outcomes.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>A calculated preference is not an operational decision.</strong> Actual
    decisions require operational eligibility, business review and verified inputs. An
    unavailable option cannot be selected merely because its number is higher.
  </p>
  <p class="caption">
    This example demonstrates a general decision-support method, not the historical
    workbook or its results. A calculated preference is not evidence of employer approval,
    implementation, adoption, savings or realized results.
  </p>
</div>
<style>
  .inventory-example .inventory-scenario {
    margin-top: 28px;
  }
  .scenario-result {
    border-left: 3px solid var(--teal);
    padding-left: 14px;
  }
</style>
